STEP 4 -- a user name the server rejects, exactly one attempt             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the far end here is the DUT itself, so a failed login counts against the unit
  under test.  AW+ locks an account after 5 failures, and every further attempt restarts the
  5-minute timer.  The refusal step therefore uses `cknosuchuser`, a user name that does not
  exist, exactly once, with no retry loop.  Zero failed `manager` logins were sent to the
  DUT at any point.

    cknosuchuser@10.38.22.34's password:
    LOGIN RESULT: FAIL  proto=ssh  user=cknosuchuser  seconds=3.0

  A refused AW+ ssh login re-presents "<user>@<host>'s password:", because the client retries
  up to three times.  That is what the tool grades as FAIL.

  The DUT's log names the user and the client:

    2026 Oct 21 00:11:18 auth.notice awplus sshd-session[11741]: Connection from 10.38.22.33 port 55486 on 10.38.22.34 port 22 rdomain ""
    2026 Oct 21 00:11:19 authpriv.notice awplus sshd-session[11752]: pam_unix(vty0:auth): check pass; user unknown
    2026 Oct 21 00:11:19 authpriv.notice awplus sshd-session[11752]: pam_unix(vty0:auth): authentication failure; logname= uid=0 euid=0 tty=ssh ruser= rhost=10.38.22.33
    2026 Oct 21 00:11:21 auth.err awplus sshd-session[11741]: error: PAM: User not known to the underlying authentication module for illegal user cknosuchuser from 10.38.22.33

  On the wire, in the same capture as STEP 3:

    flow 10.38.22.33:55486 <-> 10.38.22.34:22  packets=31  duration=2.993s  => ESTABLISHED+DATA
       10.38.22.33:55486 -> 10.38.22.34:22  pkts=14  payload=1753 B  flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, SYN}
       10.38.22.34:22 -> 10.38.22.33:55486  pkts=17  payload=1706 B  flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, SYN.ACK}

  => STEP 4 PASS: a rejected user name looks different from a served session, but NOT at the
     TCP level.  A rejected credential still reaches ESTABLISHED+DATA, because TCP, the SSH
     transport and the key exchange all complete and only the authentication fails.  The two
     flows are told apart by volume -- 8638 bytes back from the DUT in STEP 3 against 1706
     bytes here -- and by the DUT's log.  A refused service never gets past SYN, which is
     STEP 5.  Grading "did SSH work?" on ESTABLISHED alone would pass a wrong password.

STEP 5 -- `no service ssh ip`: how it is stored, and what it does         PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  This is the IPv4-off half of the case.  It asks two separate questions: how the DUT stores
  the restriction, and what the restriction does to real traffic.

awplus(config)# no service ssh ip
awplus# show ssh server
    SSH Server                         : Enabled
    Protocol                           : IPv6
    Port                               : 22
awplus# show running-config | include ssh
    ssh server allow-users manager
    service ssh ipv6

  Finding 1: SSH REPLACES the configuration line, it does not add a negation.  `service ssh`
  becomes `service ssh ipv6`, and there is no negation anywhere in the running-config.  Two
  consequences follow.  Searching a configuration for `no service ssh ip` finds NOTHING on a
  unit whose IPv4 SSH is switched off.  The state can only be read from the suffix on the
  positive line, or from the Protocol field of `show ssh server`.

  Finding 2: the log message is misleading.  Applying `no service ssh ip` makes the DUT log

    2026 Oct 21 00:12:16 user.notice awplus IMI[897]: SSH server enabled

  The word is "enabled", for a command that RESTRICTED the server.  The same line appears
  again when `service ssh ip` is restored.  Only the whole-server commands log honestly:
  "SSH server disabled" for `no service ssh`, and "SSH server enabled" for `service ssh`
  (STEP 7).  Anyone grading an SSH on/off arc from the device log alone would read it
  backwards.

  And on the wire, which is what the verdict rests on.  A fresh 30 s capture on eth3, and one
  connection attempt:

    ssh: connect to host 10.38.22.34 port 22: Connection refused

    file /tmp/ckA1159/t33380-noip.pcap: 2 packet(s) total, 1 TCP flow(s) on port 22
    flow 10.38.22.33:55488 <-> 10.38.22.34:22  packets=2  duration=0.000s  => REFUSED (SYN answered by RST)
       10.38.22.33:55488 -> 10.38.22.34:22  pkts=1  payload=0 B  flags={SYN}
       10.38.22.34:22 -> 10.38.22.33:55488  pkts=1  payload=0 B  flags={RST.ACK}

  Note on the account lockout rule: this attempt used `manager`, but it never reached
  authentication.  The TCP connection was refused and the DUT logged no sshd-session entry
  for it at all, so it cost zero failed logins.
  => STEP 5 PASS: `no service ssh ip` really closes the IPv4 half.  The DUT answered the SYN
     with RST.ACK, 2 packets in total, while `show ssh server` still read "SSH Server :
     Enabled".  That is correct behaviour -- the server is up, IPv4 is not -- and it is also
     the proof that grading this case from `show ssh server` would have passed a unit with
     the IPv4 half switched off.

