STEP 8 -- the IPv4 restriction survives a reboot                          PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: this step must not be graded from `show ssh server` or from a configuration
  search, for the two reasons STEP 5 proved.  It is graded on IPv4 TCP port 22 on the wire.
  The instrument sends a crafted SYN and reports the reply flags, where SA means the port is
  open, RA means the service refused it, and no reply row at all means the frame was
  dropped.  Every leg also carries an ICMP control to the same address, so "the DUT refused
  TCP/22" is always told apart from "the DUT was not reachable".  A port 23 control rides in
  every leg as well: telnet is off on this DUT, so port 23 must answer RA every time.  That
  control is the instrument's own witness that it decodes an RA correctly.  No credential is
  ever offered, so no leg can cost a failed login.

    sudo arpleg.py --tag <tag> --out /tmp/ntp1021/T33380 --iface eth3 \
         --tcp 10.38.22.34:22,10.38.22.34:23 --icmp 10.38.22.34 \
         --sender-ip 10.38.22.33 --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e \
         --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 4 --wait 4

  (a) Baseline, SSH on both families.

      cap t380-base-eth3.pcap: 30 frame(s)
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:22   S   [ours]
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:22    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 SA
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:22   R
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:23   S   [ours]
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:23    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 RA
        x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34      [ours]
        x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33      [ours]
        x1   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.34 tell 10.38.22.33
        x1   ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.34 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43

  4 of 4 SYNs to port 22 answered SA, so IPv4 SSH is open.  Port 23 answered RA and ICMP was
  4 of 4.

  (b) `no service ssh ip` applied.

    awplus(config)#no service ssh ip
    awplus#show running-config | include service ssh
    service ssh ipv6                       <- the line was REPLACED, not negated (§7F.10)
    awplus#show ssh server
    SSH Server : Enabled                   <- still "Enabled" (§7F.2)
    Protocol   : IPv6

      cap t380-v6only-eth3.pcap: 24 frame(s)
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:22   S   [ours]
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:22    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 RA        <- REFUSED
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:23   S   [ours]
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:23    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 RA
        x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34      [ours]
        x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33      [ours]

  Port 22 changed from SA to RA while ICMP stayed at 4 of 4.  The host is up and reachable,
  and the service is refusing.  That is the discrimination this whole step rests on.

  (c) Saved, then a cold boot, with nothing typed afterwards.

    awplus#write
    Building configuration...
    [OK]
    awplus#show startup-config | include service ssh
    service ssh ipv6                       <- saved

    tools/tb ckreload tb277 ntp1021 u2 115200 240
    17:37:54 >>> reload   (04:37:54 UTC)
    reboot question: 'reboot system? (y/n):' -> y
    17:38:54 login seen after 60 s

  The first measurement after the boot was DISCARDED, not graded.  It ran 9 seconds after the
  login prompt and returned:

      cap t380-afterboot-eth3.pcap: 16 frame(s)
        x4   TCP  ...:22  S [ours]      (no reply row at all)
        x4   TCP  ...:23  S [ours]      (no reply row at all)
        x4   ICMP t8/c0 ... [ours]
        x1   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33     <- only 1 of 4 answered
        x1   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.33 tell 10.38.22.34

  The ICMP control answered only 1 of 4, and the DUT was still sending ARP requests for the
  box, so the unit had not finished starting.  With this instrument "no reply row at all"
  means the frame was dropped, which is exactly the wrong conclusion to draw from a unit that
  is still booting.  The measurement was thrown away, the link was proved first
  (`ping -c 4` -> 4 of 4, 0% loss, average 0.442 ms), and then it was taken again.

  The graded measurement, 41 seconds after the login prompt, with NOTHING typed into the DUT:

      cap t380-afterboot2-eth3.pcap: 24 frame(s)
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:22   S   [ours]
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:22    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 RA        <- STILL REFUSED
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:23   S   [ours]
        x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:23    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 RA
        x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34      [ours]
        x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33      [ours]

